{:edb/db-before-t 872 :edb/db-after-t 873 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534185 50 #inst "2026-10-06T08:49:25.173Z" 13194139534185 true] [17592186124390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281184 13194139534185 true] [17592186124391 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281170 13194139534185 true] [17592186124391 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281177 13194139534185 true] [17592186124392 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281191 13194139534185 true] [17592186124392 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281198 13194139534185 true] [17592186281170 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281171 13194139534185 true] [17592186281170 1089 "q-108791" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281170 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534185 true] [17592186281170 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/4fec1c187ae2eaaf40461775425b31d896bdc63bc99233277d6f6bac8949315d.png)\n\nDetermine the largest domain on which the function $f(x)$ (shown above) is invertible." 13194139534185 true] [17592186281170 1113 #uuid "2716bda3-2caa-5f4f-a319-e1439728804b" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281170 1145 "First, notice that the domain of our function is $x\\in [-3,5].$ \n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ does not pass the horizontal line test on the interval $x\\in (1,3).$ In this case, the horizontal line $y=1$ intersects the graph at more than one point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/eb544795c61be7dd87568e6def10fc907c3554b603c3b344f88ac2a8ff847f8d.png)\n\n- Among the given options, the largest domain on which the function $f(x)$ is invertible is $x\\in [-3,1]\\cup (3,5].$ On this domain, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/4059a1ad684023640358af6ee38f0967957336578eb93b3299c9c842e7ce108f.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x\\in [-3,1]\\cup (3,5].$" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281172 13194139534185 true] [17592186281171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281173 13194139534185 true] [17592186281171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281174 13194139534185 true] [17592186281171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281175 13194139534185 true] [17592186281171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281176 13194139534185 true] [17592186281171 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534185 true] [17592186281171 1041 "selection" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281171 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281173 13194139534185 true] [17592186281171 1110 #uuid "b7a7b9f9-d563-50a0-9d8f-670af0c0a84d" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281172 1034 "x\\in [-3,1)" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281172 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534185 true] [17592186281172 1111 #uuid "1eca911f-15e0-5f6b-b53e-1db02e41eb41" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281173 1034 "x\\in [-3,1]\\cup (3,5]" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281173 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534185 true] [17592186281173 1111 #uuid "a5c49b51-df78-5990-a775-2cfa5e57f9d4" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281174 1034 "x\\in [0,5]" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281174 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534185 true] [17592186281174 1111 #uuid "2a01ee71-9ec1-5530-b7d1-2c75d5bb18c8" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281175 1034 "x\\in [1,5]" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281175 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534185 true] [17592186281175 1111 #uuid "8a60fc37-39f8-5c52-a0c1-ffc572b55663" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281176 1034 "x\\in [-3,5]" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281176 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534185 true] [17592186281176 1111 #uuid "4be355c9-6241-56c4-be75-006c32421cdf" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281177 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281178 13194139534185 true] [17592186281177 1089 "q-36066" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281177 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534185 true] [17592186281177 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/e2065775ae6372e8c820b5e2f19f6f105e032dcd37db1e32e04af5bfa6a4faf9.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-3,-2]\\cup (2,4)$\n- $x\\in (0,2]$\n- $x\\in [1,4)$" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281177 1113 #uuid "e45c5810-f668-5215-947d-cd789baa0e33" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281177 1145 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The given function is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-3,-2]\\cup (2,4)$ and $x\\in (0,2].$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/3c5bc724ee29aee7bef2790012bf0e73ce1ed087134213dbad36a670fd136a36.png)\n\n        \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $[1,4)$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/03b9d70513a4ef99b39c9f05d0c0ac803f8e5611d0967015e9c1d9c0f45bd418.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  and II only\"." 13194139534185 true] [17592186281178 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281179 13194139534185 true] [17592186281178 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281180 13194139534185 true] [17592186281178 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281181 13194139534185 true] [17592186281178 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281182 13194139534185 true] [17592186281178 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281183 13194139534185 true] [17592186281178 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534185 true] [17592186281178 1041 "selection" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281178 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281182 13194139534185 true] [17592186281178 1110 #uuid "6db0ba1f-c716-505f-8a29-660727fab7b7" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281179 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281179 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534185 true] [17592186281179 1111 #uuid "03ac8e54-e340-519a-b937-7f87cc542fa0" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281180 1034 "I only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281180 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534185 true] [17592186281180 1111 #uuid "4b1dcf4e-7f52-529b-b2c3-230d08e0d5f6" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281181 1034 "III only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534185 true] [17592186281181 1111 #uuid "91caee2f-5e5d-5b2f-82a7-65ea098c4d94" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281182 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281182 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534185 true] [17592186281182 1111 #uuid "3674cb89-f064-5d4c-84fd-a9bd57b33590" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281183 1034 "II only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281183 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534185 true] [17592186281183 1111 #uuid "86988d5a-e4f0-5c43-b586-3be5586018eb" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281184 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281185 13194139534185 true] [17592186281184 1089 "q-66508" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281184 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534185 true] [17592186281184 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/239490fd3be930412d4f67ff89244607a5019d91f201bc4e579410b5610a18ed.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281184 1113 #uuid "5b9771ce-36d1-5cb6-a7ee-84e40d892ce6" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281184 1145 "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/c728d173c13a1e7b3bcad0e9f0284c97f2ca075c795dcb1f38ed086c19cc0504.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- For graphs I and IV, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graphs I  and IV show one-to-one functions. This means that  these functions are invertible.\n\n- For graphs II and  III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible." 13194139534185 true] [17592186281185 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281186 13194139534185 true] [17592186281185 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281187 13194139534185 true] [17592186281185 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281188 13194139534185 true] [17592186281185 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281189 13194139534185 true] [17592186281185 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281190 13194139534185 true] [17592186281185 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534185 true] [17592186281185 1041 "selection" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281185 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281186 13194139534185 true] [17592186281185 1110 #uuid "1ba75589-e05a-5239-ba38-5d369acdfe1b" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281186 1034 "I and IV only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281186 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534185 true] [17592186281186 1111 #uuid "f0ac737c-41e6-57d0-ba16-51c7cc2f443e" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281187 1034 "II and IV only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281187 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534185 true] [17592186281187 1111 #uuid "b3133d26-0910-5726-8a2f-05da4273b9f7" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281188 1034 "I only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281188 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534185 true] [17592186281188 1111 #uuid "ebb79a7c-8bc5-5fbb-90ee-12feef9b009f" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281189 1034 "III only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281189 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534185 true] [17592186281189 1111 #uuid "804228d1-bba3-5d0d-8aed-4778eb148c71" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281190 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281190 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534185 true] [17592186281190 1111 #uuid "5a3ccd88-5d7a-5b7d-b18d-fe4beaef1415" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281191 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281192 13194139534185 true] [17592186281191 1089 "q-66687" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281191 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534185 true] [17592186281191 1100 "Given that $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0]$\n- $x\\in (-2,2]$\n- $x\\in (-2,\\infty )$" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281191 1113 #uuid "a7409414-1d50-5e61-8a1d-1279f92552ae" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281191 1145 "The graph of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+1$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Reflect its graph across  the $x$-axis to get $y=-{x}^{2}.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=-{x}^{2}$  by $1$ unit up to  get $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/83a9f5966c76b89b2cad9c6244e2e447fb0052fc7f946df954df76265628c4a7.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n*The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,0].$ On this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/f694b6b1bc979289b748758385e05575990bd866457e75a84fbf7bdf335c03b9.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,2].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once. Since  $(-2,2]$ is a subset of $(-2,\\infty )$, our function is not invertible on $x\\in (-2,\\infty )$ either.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/c03118f3fd0193910b49e26ab5ea56e4320fd23583feb3267cd4442d0a157583.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139534185 true] [17592186281192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281193 13194139534185 true] [17592186281192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281194 13194139534185 true] [17592186281192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281195 13194139534185 true] [17592186281192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281196 13194139534185 true] [17592186281192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281197 13194139534185 true] [17592186281192 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534185 true] [17592186281192 1041 "selection" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281192 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281195 13194139534185 true] [17592186281192 1110 #uuid "7b4a97d1-8d72-53a5-b1c5-01ba48eced9d" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281193 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281193 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534185 true] [17592186281193 1111 #uuid "e9351ec5-6765-5e0e-a20d-1a25f3c06a21" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281194 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281194 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534185 true] [17592186281194 1111 #uuid "007672c4-f15d-53f4-b67b-c8af50920222" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281195 1034 "I only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281195 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534185 true] [17592186281195 1111 #uuid "8a8e9afb-b4a8-58b8-baba-921b8e8b0c56" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281196 1034 "III only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281196 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534185 true] [17592186281196 1111 #uuid "60e85c31-8c87-567b-849f-954bb4fb06b9" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281197 1034 "II only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281197 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534185 true] [17592186281197 1111 #uuid "84280a1a-1ab5-5b55-a9c0-432970edf934" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281198 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281199 13194139534185 true] [17592186281198 1089 "q-66724" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281198 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534185 true] [17592186281198 1100 "Given that $f(x)=2x+4,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (10,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in [-5,5)$" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281198 1113 #uuid "8061fba9-94fb-59ab-bb67-c981e34d25e7" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281198 1145 "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $2$ and $y$-intercept $4,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/d33314230419ec762bc4ad8603b2e6c3436461cd6b4b6fe4783d12a23d01b660.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (10,\\infty )$ and $x\\in [-5,5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139534185 true] [17592186281199 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281200 13194139534185 true] [17592186281199 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281201 13194139534185 true] [17592186281199 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281202 13194139534185 true] [17592186281199 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281203 13194139534185 true] [17592186281199 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281204 13194139534185 true] [17592186281199 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534185 true] [17592186281199 1041 "selection" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281199 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281203 13194139534185 true] [17592186281199 1110 #uuid "3714d6a9-12e2-54c9-bcd8-69a352004d8e" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281200 1034 "I only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281200 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534185 true] [17592186281200 1111 #uuid "a8663344-7d91-546c-a7f1-bc287977dcaf" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281201 1034 "III only" 13194139534185 true] [17592186281201 1103 #edb/ref 1759218604